  :doc/body "# Codegen co-scientist, iteration 32

The rematch iteration 31 licensed: it said \"do not rematch `kernel_deep`
until a pin changes the extracted bytes.\" The pins moved a lot -- amu +306,
kotoba-native +122, kotoba-mir +55 -- and the extracted leaf went 1068 B to
964 B. So the bytes changed and this is that rematch.

Do not overwrite 04-31. Do not rematch `kernel_wide` (iteration 14).
Do not ship rust sources into amu. Do not ship a timed bench export.

## One thing is better than iteration 31

Iteration 31 had to compile the LLVM arm on a loaded workstation because
judah had no rustc, and timed on judah. Judah has rustc now. **Both arms
were built and timed on the same quiet host.** That removes a cross-machine
compile from the comparison.

## What blocked it first, and is the more important finding

`amu compile kernel_deep.kotoba --target aarch64-macos` **fails with
`:kotoba/internal-error` on the nbb route**, which is the default route for
native targets and the only JVM-free one.

It is not a regression and not the environment. Discriminated four ways:

| input | route | result |
|---|---|---|
| `tiny.kotoba` -> aarch64-macos | nbb | ok |
| `kernel_deep.kotoba` -> wasm32 | nbb | ok |
| `kernel_deep.kotoba` -> aarch64-macos | nbb | **internal compiler error** |
| `kernel_deep.kotoba` -> aarch64-macos | JVM (`clojure -M:run`) | ok, 6986 B kexe, 964 B extracted |

Reproduced at amu `f403ed29` (main) and at `27d69898` (iteration 31's own
pin), on judah and on the workstation. `--jvm-free` fails identically, so
the route Q9 depends on cannot build this program at all. The frontend is
fine -- the same source compiles to wasm32 -- so this is the AArch64 native
lowering on one route only.

Two implementations of one command disagree on one input. The timing below
therefore ran on the **JVM-built** artifact, as iteration 31's must have.

## What was timed

| arm | build | bytes | sha256 |
|---|---|---:|---|
| amu | JVM route, then `extract-native --symbol kernel`, offset 0 | **964** | `e47f8e6c5709bfa11ee0e4c635c9cab282b2f272c0b7154a198397c078a22f42` |
| llvm | `rustc 1.98.0 --edition 2021 -C opt-level=3 -C codegen-units=1 --crate-type=cdylib` | 16792 | `e86a208de01129879c3b10b4325eea53d67e3c298af01207ec328f0d629ec2d0` |

Runner rebuilt on judah from `bench/runtime-comparison/kexe-benchmark.c` at
amu main (the isa is already hoisted there, so this is the iteration-29
runner). Iteration 31 reused a `/tmp` binary; `/tmp` had been cleared, so
this one was rebuilt rather than reused.

## Oracle

One-shot, 1 call and 1 warmup, both arms: **2552249090**, the same value
iteration 31 recorded, and it held on every one of the 50 scored samples.
STOP was not taken.

## Times

12 discarded frequency-warmup rotations, then 25 scored rotations, arms
rotated ABBA. n=200, 100k calls, 10k warmup, fuel 1048576.
judah loadavg 2.28 at start and 2.28 at end.

| arm | min | mean | median | p95 | max | rel stdev |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| amu | 8.94 | 8.9732 | 8.97 | 9.07 | 9.11 | 0.0045 |
| llvm | 8.71 | 8.7680 | 8.74 | 8.95 | 8.95 | 0.0084 |

**min ratio amu/llvm 1.0264.** Mean improvement -2.34%.

perfgate refused: `:improvement-below-threshold` (requires +5%). The gap of
0.2052 ns exceeds the summed standard deviation of 0.1140, so the arms ARE
separated -- the difference is real and it is in LLVM's favour.
`:llvm-beaten` remains **false**. No claim artifact.

amu's relative standard deviation (0.0045) is roughly half LLVM's (0.0084).
Steadier, still behind.

## What this does and does not establish

Established, on this kernel, this host, these pins: amu is 1.0264x LLVM at
the minimum and 2.34% behind at the mean, separated from noise.

**Not established: that amu got 8.5% faster.** The amu arm moved 9.77 ->
8.94 ns since iteration 31, but three things changed at once -- the pins,
the rustc version (1.97.1 -> 1.98.0), and the LLVM arm's build host (a
loaded Air -> judah). The LLVM arm moved too, 8.928 -> 8.71. The ratio is
the quantity this run measured; a cross-iteration delta on either arm alone
mixes changes and is not claimed.

## Next

The bar is not parity. perfgate's default policy needs **+5%** to seal a
\"faster\" claim, so amu must be 5% faster than LLVM here, not 2.6% behind.
The remaining 0.205 ns/call is the thing to rank.

Do not rematch `kernel_deep` until a pin changes the extracted bytes again.
The nbb native-route failure is the blocker to fix first: while it stands,
the default and JVM-free routes cannot build the fixture this loop ranks,
and every future rematch has to be hand-routed through the JVM.
